Q: The browser asks for Google Sign-in each time I open or reload the page - how to fix? A: <p>I am assuming since you are getting the behaviour which you want to avoid, you would have already debugged the issue, what does the code debugging shows, what are the missing values. Clearly <code>gapi.client.getToken()</code> is leading to null value, leading to <code>requestAccessToken()</code>call on every reload, thus sign in on every reload.</p>
<p>Here's my understanding:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>On the loading  / reloading when it calls the functions <code>gapiloaded() and gisloaded() </code>, which will call the <code>maybeAuthorize() and authorize()</code>. The core issue is that the Google Identity Services (GIS) token client uses <strong>in-memory tokens only</strong> — they're lost on every page reload. And <code>gapi.client.getToken()</code> will always return <code>null</code> on a fresh load, so your code always hits <code>requestAccessToken()</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The <code>prompt: ''</code> trick for silent auth only works if the user already has an active Google session <em>and</em> has previously consented, but even then it often still flashes the account picker or fails entirely depending on browser cookie state.</p>
<p>Here's what you should do: <strong>persist the token to <code>sessionStorage</code></strong> and restore it on load</p>
<pre><code>// After a successful token response, save it:
callback: async (resp) =&gt; {
  if (resp.error) {
    console.error(resp);
    return;
  }
  // Save the token so it survives reloads
  sessionStorage.setItem('gapi_token', JSON.stringify(gapi.client.getToken()));
  await createAllContent();
},
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Then in your <code>authorize()</code> function, try restoring the saved token first:</p>
<pre><code>function authorize() {
  // Try restoring a saved token
  const saved = sessionStorage.getItem('gapi_token');
  if (saved) {
    const token = JSON.parse(saved);
    // Check if it's still valid (not expired)
    const expiresAt = token.expires_at || 0;
    if (Date.now() &lt; expiresAt * 1000) {
      gapi.client.setToken(token);
      createAllContent();
      return;
    } else {
      // Token expired, clear it
      sessionStorage.removeItem('gapi_token');
    }
  }

  // No valid saved token — request a new one
  tokenClient.requestAccessToken({ prompt: '' });
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>A few things to keep in mind:</p>
<p><strong>Token expiry</strong> — Google access tokens typically last 1 hour. The <code>expires_at</code> field might not always be present depending on the response format. A more robust approach is to save <code>Date.now() + (resp.expires_in * 1000)</code> alongside the token when you first receive it, then check against that.</p>
<p><strong><code>sessionStorage</code> vs <code>localStorage</code></strong> — <code>sessionStorage</code> clears when the tab closes, which is a reasonable security tradeoff for access tokens. If you want persistence across browser sessions, you could use <code>localStorage</code>, but be aware that access tokens are short-lived anyway, so you'd mostly be avoiding re-auth within the same hour.</p>
<p><strong>Handling token expiry gracefully</strong> — you'll also want to wrap your API calls so that if one fails with a 401, you clear the stored token and re-trigger <code>authorize()</code>. Something like:</p>
<pre><code>async function createAllContent() {
  try {
    // your API calls...
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.status === 401) {
      sessionStorage.removeItem('gapi_token');
      authorize();
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>
<p>This way, sign-in only happens once per session (or once per hour when the token expires), and reloads just pick up the saved token silently. I hope this helps in mitigating the issue to an extent.</p>

Q: I am in search of assistance A: <p>Okay, so you have a problem related to C# (.NET) coding and while you did not share your code, you said you have difficulty in converting into string. This is not a Visual Studio problem, as Visual Studio is an IDE which aims to help you writing code, but it is not responsible for the code itself. This is the quadratic formula:</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/DdtuSvq4.png" alt="(-b +- sqrt(b^2-4ac)) / 2a" /></p>
<p>Now, your input looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>aX^2 + bX + c = 0
</code></pre>
<p>Let's first understand the problem</p>
<ol>
<li>If a = 0 and b &lt;&gt; 0, then the solution will be x = -c / b</li>
<li>If b = 0 and a &lt;&gt; 0, then the solution will be x = +-sqrt(c/a)</li>
<li>If a = 0, b = 0 and c = 0, then the solution is any X possible, as 0X^2 + 0X + 0 = 0</li>
<li>If a = 0, b = 0 and c &lt;&gt; 0, then the equation makes no sense</li>
<li>We assume that the equation has 0 on the right-hand-side, so if you had aX^2 + bX + c1 = c2, then c = c1 - c2</li>
<li>When you compute sqrt of a negative number, then you need to keep in mind that sqrt(-|a^2|) = i * a, where i is the imaginary number</li>
</ol>
<p>With all this in mind, this could be a solution to apply, of course, you may get rounding issues, which you can fix on your own, as it's outside the scope of this question, but you can see from here already how we are converting string received from console into numbers and back:</p>
<pre><code>using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        double a, b, c;
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;a = &quot;);
        a = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;b = &quot;);
        b = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;c = &quot;);
        c = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());
        Console.WriteLine(a);
        if ((a == 0) || (b == 0)) {
            if ((a == 0) &amp;&amp; (b == 0)) {
                if (c == 0) {
                    Console.WriteLine(&quot;Any X fulfills 0 * X^2 + 0 * X + 0 = 0&quot;);
                } else {
                    Console.WriteLine(&quot;The equation does not make sense&quot;);
                }
            } else if (a == 0) {
                Console.WriteLine(&quot;X = &quot; + (-c / b));
            } else {
                double dividend = -c / a;
                bool positive = dividend &gt;= 0;
                if (dividend == 0) {
                    Console.WriteLine(&quot;X = 0&quot;);
                } else {
                    Console.WriteLine(&quot;X1,2 = +- &quot; + Math.Sqrt(positive ? dividend : -dividend) + (positive ? &quot;&quot; : &quot;* i&quot;));
                }
            }
        } else {
            double delta = b * b - (4 * a * c);
            bool positive = delta &gt;= 0;
            double sqrtDelta = Math.Sqrt(positive ? delta : -delta);
            if (positive) {
                double solution1 = (-b - sqrtDelta) / (2 * a);
                double solution2 = (-b + sqrtDelta) / (2 * a);
                if (solution1 == solution2) {
                    Console.WriteLine(&quot;X = &quot; + solution1);
                } else {
                    Console.WriteLine(&quot;X1 = &quot; + solution1 + &quot;, X2 = &quot; + solution2);
                }
            } else {
                Console.WriteLine(&quot;X1,2 = &quot; + (-b / (2 * a)) + &quot; +- i * &quot; + (sqrtDelta / (2 * a)));
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Combine flows but always wait for one to emit A: <pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>combine(flowA, flowB, ::Pair).mapLatest { (a, b) -&gt; Triple(a, b, transform(a, b)) }
</code></pre>
<p>using <code>.mapLatest</code> we guarantee, that the consumer will receive the most recent calculated value from <code>transform</code>, which means, that source values are also the most recent ones</p>
